          %title Contents with links to Lab pages
          %p This entry is to record the reference
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                \:
              %p
                %br/
              %p
                %strong Stone Spaces
              %p
                %br/
              %p
                Studies in Advanced Mathematics
                %strong 3
              %p Cambridge University Press (1982, 1986)
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://www.cambridge.org/de/academic/subjects/mathematics/logic-categories-and-sets/stone-spaces?format=PB&isbn=9780521337793"} ISBN:9780521337793
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=698074"} MR85f:54002
          %p
            ultimately on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+representation+theorem"} Stone representation theorem
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointfree+topology"} pointfree topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            in place of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
          %p
            (The same author wrote
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topos+Theory"} Topos Theory
            1977 and the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
          %p
            Although it is a work of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            rather than
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} metamathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            even when analogous results about topological spaces do.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Taylor"} Paul Taylor
            has written of this book that:
          %blockquote
            %p
              the public theorems about topology […] are marked with an asterisk, although the official meaning of that symbol is a dependence on the axiom of choice. &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.monad.me.uk/ASD/dedras/"}> ASD I
              , p. 3&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            but, unfortunately for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematicians
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            is
            %em not
            considered a form of choice by Johnstone.
          %p A trailer for the book (according to its own words) is
          %ul
            %li#Johnstone83
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
              ,
              %em The point of pointless topology
              , Bull. Amer. Math. Soc. (N.S.) Volume 8, Number 1 (1983), 41-53. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/bull/1983-08-01/S0273-0979-1983-15080-2/home.html"}> Bulletin AMS
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.bams/1183550014"} Euclid
          %h2#contents_with_links_to_lab_pages
            Contents with links to
            = succeed "Lab" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Besides the usual prefaces, bibliography, and indexes, there is a historical introduction, and each chapter concludes with notes on historical and metamathematical aspects. Otherwise, each of 7 chapters is divided into 4 sections, which in turn contain paragraphs that deal with essentially one idea each. For the moment, we list (with minimal processing) the definitions from the index in each section. There will also be some summaries of theorems; as in the book itself, an asterisk here indicates dependence on some form of choice beyond excluded middle (more precisely, a proof that cannot be internalised in an arbitrary
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+topos"} boolean topos
          %ol
